      "writtenOn": "2013-12-03T17:08:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, this can cause problems. For example, Manager.remove_user_from_project makes a bunch of queries in separate transactions to get all of the roles a user has on a tenant, then calls the driver\u0027s remove_role_from_user_and_project on each role. Since a role could be deleted between driver calls, the Manager\u0027s remove_user_from_project can return a 404 even though not all of the roles have been deleted: for example, the second role in a list of five is deleted in another transaction, then the last 3 roles aren\u0027t deleted.\n\nThere are a couple of solutions to this problem. One would be to add try: except: around driver calls that could refer to data that can be deleted:\n\n    def remove_user_from_project(...)\n    ...\n        for role_id in roles:\n            try:\n                self.driver.remove_role_from_user_and_project(user_id, tenant_id,\n                                                          role_id)\n            except RoleNotFound:\n                pass\n\nAnother solution would be to add begin \u0026 end transaction methods to the driver interface and call them at the higher level spots in the manager. A simple begin \u0026 end transaction implementation could be to serialize everything with a lock; for sql, we\u0027d obviously use database transactions.\n\nI\u0027m pretty sure the try: except: approach would be a smaller patch, so that\u0027s what I\u0027d favor implementing.",
      "parentUuid": "AAAATn/+ADw\u003d",
      "revId": "8dd9851e66a4fc6021ef88648869842d88d922ce",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
